WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.772 --> 00:00:04.381
(crickets chirping)

00:00:04.381 --> 00:00:08.382
- [Voiceover] Every place
in the world sounds unique.

00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:13.181
These soundscapes tell us
a story about that place.

00:00:13.181 --> 00:00:15.925
They tell us the ways
in which global warming

00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:19.758
is beginning to change
the natural soundscape.

00:00:24.864 --> 00:00:27.501
Just before the sun rises at first light

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:30.205
is the dawn course.

00:00:30.205 --> 00:00:32.517
(nature sounds)

00:00:32.517 --> 00:00:34.991
The birds begin to sing and the insects

00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:36.549
and the amphibians.

00:00:36.549 --> 00:00:39.629
And that's the time of day
to go out and listen.

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:41.966
I'm a soundscape ecologist

00:00:41.966 --> 00:00:44.057
and I record animals for a living.

00:00:44.057 --> 00:00:46.729
This is Sugarloaf State Park.

00:00:46.729 --> 00:00:49.273
Every spring, I can
probably be found there

00:00:49.273 --> 00:00:52.058
once a week recording in the same spot

00:00:52.058 --> 00:00:54.297
using the same kind of
equipment so that I could

00:00:54.297 --> 00:00:56.345
repeat these recordings
over and over again

00:00:56.345 --> 00:00:57.678
and compare them.

00:01:01.225 --> 00:01:04.002
And I have quite an archive of material

00:01:04.002 --> 00:01:06.906
which shows, over time,
this habitat has been

00:01:06.906 --> 00:01:10.593
changing pretty radically as
a result of global warming

00:01:10.593 --> 00:01:12.961
and the California drought.

00:01:12.961 --> 00:01:15.711
(birds chirping)

00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:21.850
The first recording made
in 2004 shows a very robust

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:24.385
habitat with a signature of a stream.

00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:27.052
(nature sounds)

00:01:29.808 --> 00:01:32.298
In 2014, things were changing radically.

00:01:32.298 --> 00:01:34.513
It was already the second
year of a major drought

00:01:34.513 --> 00:01:35.680
in California.

00:01:39.393 --> 00:01:43.560
And then 2015, we had what
I call a silent spring.

00:01:47.281 --> 00:01:50.385
Over 50 percent of my
collection comes from habitats

00:01:50.385 --> 00:01:53.073
that are so radically
altered they're either

00:01:53.073 --> 00:01:55.976
altogether silent or
can no longer be heard

00:01:55.976 --> 00:01:57.776
in their original form.

00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:01.194
Here's an example from Costa Rica.

00:02:01.194 --> 00:02:03.861
(nature sounds)

00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:08.698
That's before logging, this is after.

00:02:09.997 --> 00:02:12.247
(soft hum)

00:02:16.902 --> 00:02:18.149
Same spot.

00:02:18.149 --> 00:02:21.917
This is a coral reef, Vanua Levu in Fiji.

00:02:21.917 --> 00:02:23.901
Part of it is dying and
part of it is still living.

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:26.092
Here's what the living part sounds like.

00:02:26.092 --> 00:02:28.675
(water sounds)

00:02:30.789 --> 00:02:34.039
And here's what the dying part sounds like.

00:02:36.256 --> 00:02:38.389
Hardly any fish sounds.

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:40.429
(calm instrumental)

00:02:40.429 --> 00:02:44.798
We really don't know what
this means in the long run.

00:02:44.798 --> 00:02:48.230
In the short run, we're beginning
to lose these soundscapes

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:50.861
because the habitats are just changing so radically

00:02:50.861 --> 00:02:54.542
and we're partly to blame for that.

00:02:54.542 --> 00:02:57.310
When I first began to
record, almost 50 years ago,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.246
I would go out into the
field and just put on a pair

00:02:59.246 --> 00:03:01.644
of earphones and sit and listen

00:03:01.644 --> 00:03:04.293
because it made me feel good.

00:03:04.293 --> 00:03:07.647
It made me aware of the
living world around me,

00:03:07.647 --> 00:03:10.841
and so I want to do anything
that does that for me.

00:03:10.841 --> 00:03:13.663
(nature sounds)

00:03:13.663 --> 00:03:16.663
(calm instrumental)

